|> how would the future perfect and subjunctives go in E'?
|> (He makes a bold stab at guessing the name of the tense he was using...)
|> Rich!

You can unfold passive voice by identifying the correct subject and
using an active construction:

"The use of E' would perhaps facilitate list communication."

Strunk and White also recommend this in _The Elements of Style_.

E' "moderates" developed "E-choice" (named by analogy with USDA meat
grades ;-) to distinguish the use of "to be" forms for progressive and
perfect tenses from expressions of identity, claiming that the former
do not cause the same problems as the latter.

I haven't finished my homework on this yet, but I've learned a lot
from the glossy handout from ISGS and the sample copy of _Etc._

Subjunctive forms do not cause a problem in E', as far as I know.
