From: Michael Clive Price <price@price.demon.co.uk>
Subject: Meta: Judgment Metzger vs. Moore

I agree with Mark DeSilets that the reputation of the list suffers from
bad judgments, such as has been rendered in the Ms Moore case.  I was
surprised and flabbergasted when Harry speculated about whether Tim May
_should_ have filed charges against Carol and, having decided he should
have, proceeded to punish her, partly, on that basis.  Uarrgh.  Just my
opinion, of course.

In an earlier post Perry mentions that anyone can file charges, but too
few do.  This is because in the past there have been cases where the
filer of charges has been rather unfairly 'slapped in the face' (again
IMHO).  Remember Dave Krieger?  He stopped posting (and probably left
the list) after another harsh judgment.  We lost a valuable contributor
there.  And Dave was complaining about, if not Pandit (although I think
it was), then some equally horrible character!

The expulsion of Pandit was quite justified, and perhaps even overdue.
His non-scientific views on flying yogis are incompatible with list
basics, although he, in the end, was expelled for other reasons.

To be blunt, I think the mechanism for list justice stinks - the rules
against flames are being interpreted both too legalistically and
(ironically) subjectively.  If someone had filed charges during the
early days of the latest Objectivist wars we'd have never got the more
recent thoughtful and (IMHO) acutely perceptive pieces from Eric.  

Harry says he wants to encourage a market lead justice system.  In my
opinion we do not have that.  We have a Harry Shapiro justice system,
and I do not like it.  All forms of justice have to be subjective.  I'd
rather throw myself upon the subjective judgment of an ExI committee
than Harry.  Sorry Harry, I think you're doing a great job as list
system administrator, but you clearly haven't the time for judgments. 
I'd rather an committee administered justice than an single, overworked
administrator.  Let some committee form value judgments (eg flying yogis
suck!) and 'arbitrarily' boot people off (after warnings), as Perry
says.  I'd have more confidence in such a system than the present
mechanism.

