>levels, and also consensus functions relating CO2 levels to weather
>variability and sea level rise.  Richard Sandor's catastrophe futures 
>(based on future payouts on catastrophe insurance, eg for hurricanes, 
>flooding, etc.) can then be used to estimate specific damages.

Hmm.. if they were legal I would be optimistic that betting markets
could give us decent estimates of various broad consequences,
conditional on various levels of C02 in the air.  And maybe similar
markets could estimate who/where is actually emitting how much CO2,
and who/where is absorbing it.  But the real question is, what can we
*do* with this information?

Yes in principle those who emit would pay damages to those harmed by
CO2, and would get compensation from those who benefit.  But since
this is such a global thing, it is very hard to imagine a decent deal
actually being struck.  Those emitting/absorbing and being helped/hurt
may not be well defined groups with political clout or ability to be
paid/taxed.  But if the problem is actually serious, let us hope, and
work toward, at least a crude deal.

Robin Hanson

